//==
// # Counted work (complexity sweep, docs/plans/step-mesher-complexity.md)
//
// The three types the operation-count laws use (testing strategy, part b): a
// function's value beside the work it counted, and a claim that work is at
// most a slope times the size, measured at two or more sizes. One law, Law_WorkIsWithinSlope, reads every
// WorkClaim witness; a chunk adds a Witness_<Family>Work, never a law.
//
// Sections, in order:
//  - Counted values
//  - Claims

//==
// # Counted values

// The value a function computed and the work it counted doing so: its own
// evaluations, copies, flips and projections, not the transitive total.
// Removes: a complexity claim with no number behind it.
type Counted<T>
{
    Value: T;
    Work: WorkBudget;
}

//==
// # Claims

// A claim that a function's work is at most Slope per unit of size, with the
// means to check it: WorkAt measures the function at a size, and Sizes names
// two or more growing sizes of one witness family, so growth is visible. The
// function-typed field keeps the law runner from generating claims: only the
// Witness_*Work values are its cases. Invariant: Sizes.Count >= 2.
type WorkClaim
{
    Family: String;
    Slope: WorkBudget;
    Sizes: Array<Integer>;
    WorkAt: Function1<Integer, WorkBudget>;
}
